{"jsonrpc":"2.0","t":50237,"method":"harness","params":{"epoch":"f941abb68d58","items":[{"anchor":{"bytes":60192,"epoch":"74035fd36193a0b8","line":20,"needle":"❯"},"assistant":{"partial":false,"text":"`POST /login` now allows 5 attempts per minute per IP and returns `429` after that. `npm test` passes: 9 tests across 3 files, including the 5 new ones.\n\n- **Limiter:** `src/rateLimit.ts` is a fixed-window, in-memory limiter keyed on `req.ip`. It adds no dependencies. `src/app.ts` puts it in front of `login`.\n- **429 response:** the sixth attempt gets `429 {error: \"too many attempts, try again later\"}` plus a `Retry-After` header.\n- **Counting:** every attempt counts, successful or not, so correct credentials are also blocked once the limit is hit. The counter resets when the window expires.\n- **Tests:**\n  - The sixth attempt is blocked, with the `Retry-After` header.\n  - Correct credentials are blocked once the limit is hit.\n  - Attempts work again after the window passes, using fake timers.\n  - `/health` is not limited.\n  - A direct middleware test shows each IP is counted separately.\n\nThings to know:\n- **Behind a proxy:** `req.ip` is the proxy's address unless you set `app.set(\"trust proxy\", ...)`. Without that, every client shares one bucket.\n- **Counts are not shared:** they live in memory per process, so they reset on restart and aren't shared across multiple instances.\n- **Fixed window:** a client can make up to 10 attempts around a window boundary (5 at the end of one window, 5 at the start of the next).\n- **Per-IP only:** it limits per IP, not per account, so a botnet could still target one email.\n\nI haven't committed anything."},"at":"2026-11-08T13:07:56.934Z","id":"msg_011CgqWhdCNpJYCeSZBFQeRj","kind":"assistant","seq":31,"turnId":"2b93d2d1-a3bd-48c6-9868-3ddf3e635dd9"}],"kind":"items","sessionId":"s_viqfwhqaoo","ts":"2026-11-08T08:07:57.422602-05:00"}}
